(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-injury"]={"plain":{"bills":{"injury":{"slug":"injury","title":"The Patient Safety and Compensation Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short title","part":null,"body":"This Act may be cited as The Patient Safety and Compensation Act."},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings","part":null,"body":"The Congress finds the following:\n\n**(a) Findings.** The Congress finds the following:\n\n**(1) The system for compensating medical injury reaches almost none of the people it exists for.** A study of 30,121 hospital records found that adverse events occurred in 3.7 percent of hospitalizations and that 27.6 percent of those were due to negligence; of 280 patients injured by negligent care, eight filed a claim. The data are from 1984 and from one State, and the Congress states them as such.\n\n**(2) The barrier is economic and not legal.** A claim requires expert testimony and years of litigation, so it must be worth several hundred thousand dollars before counsel will take it. **Every injured person below that line is uncompensated, and they are the great majority.**\n\n**(3) The barrier falls hardest on those with the least to lose in earnings.** A retiree, a child, a person raising a family at home, and a person already out of the workforce have small economic damages however severe the injury, and are therefore least able to obtain counsel.\n\n**(4) Litigation destroys the information that would prevent the next injury.** Candor becomes a liability, most claims resolve under confidentiality, and the institution learns nothing that others can use.\n\n**(5) Administrative compensation for medical injury is not novel.** Four Nordic countries, New Zealand and Japan have operated such systems for decades. Within the United States, the Congress has established administrative compensation for vaccine injury, for injury arising from care at Department of Veterans Affairs facilities, and for injury to members of the uniformed services.\n\n**(6) Those systems compensate more people at lower administrative cost.** In one such system, administrative overhead was approximately 18 percent of premium, and more than 80 percent of premium reached injured patients as compensation, against approximately 40 percent under the United States malpractice system. The comparison is from 1992.\n\n**(7) The standard of avoidability is already known to Federal law.** Section 1886(d)(4)(D) of the Social Security Act conditions payment on whether a hospital-acquired condition **could reasonably have been prevented through the application of evidence-based guidelines.**\n\n**(8) An administrative system in which the paying party also decides is not a solution.** Under one Federal program in which the Department that provides the care also adjudicates claims against it, fewer than ten percent of claims have been approved.\n\n**(9) A compensation system that pays only lump sums leaves the catastrophically injured to exhaust them and become dependent on public programs.** Two State programs accumulated substantial assets while shifting the cost of care to Medicaid.\n\n**(10) Disclosure increases the number of claims and reduces their cost.** A Federal medical center that adopted full disclosure and compensation in 1987 ranked in the highest quartile of such centers for claims filed and the lowest quartile for amounts paid.\n\n**(b) Purpose.** The purpose of this Section is **to compensate people injured by avoidable medical care, promptly and without proof of fault; to generate and publish the record of how those injuries occurred so that they are not repeated; and to do both well enough that injured people choose this Section over litigation.**\n\n**(c) Replacement by competition, not by prohibition.** **This Section forecloses no remedy.** It conditions the time at which a civil action may be brought and requires a person who brings one to forgo compensation under this Section. **The Congress intends that the great majority of injured people will choose this Section because it serves them better \u2014 sooner, more certainly, without deduction for fees, and without regard to whether their injury cost them wages.** **Where it does not serve them better, the courthouse remains open, and the proportion who decline is published under \u00a7 10-14(e) so that the Congress and the public may judge whether it is working.**\n\n**(d) The hospital-acquired condition reduction program.** The Congress finds that the Medicare Payment Advisory Commission recommended that the four programs for measuring and rewarding hospital quality, including the hospital-acquired condition reduction program, **be replaced by a single program**; that the hospital-acquired condition reduction program penalizes a fixed quartile of hospitals without regard to whether their performance differs; and that hospitals serving disadvantaged populations and hospitals engaged in teaching are penalized at higher rates, safety-net hospitals with major teaching levels being **twice as likely** to be penalized as non-safety-net nonteaching hospitals. **The Congress further finds that this Section reduces no payment to which any individual is entitled under title XVIII and alters no obligation of the Federal Hospital Insurance Trust Fund: it ends a reduction in payments to hospitals and applies the same amounts to compensating patients injured in the course of care.**\n\n**(e) Authority.** This Section is enacted under the power of the Congress to regulate commerce among the several States, health care being an economic activity conducted across State lines, and, as to any provision held not to be sustained by that power, under the power to provide for the general welfare through conditions on Federal payment."},{"anchor":"sec-10-1","heading":"\u00a7 10-1. Definitions","part":null,"body":"In this Section:\n\n**(a) Avoidable injury.** An injury to a patient arising from health care is **avoidable** if it could have been avoided had the care been delivered in accordance with the highest standard of care and expertise reasonably attainable at the time of the care, assessed with knowledge of the outcome.\n\n**(b) Rule of construction as to fault.** A determination that an injury was avoidable is **not** a determination that any person breached a standard of care, acted negligently, or is liable to any person. The existence and content of the right created by this Section do not depend on the construction any court has given to any provision of law governing negligence, malpractice, or professional liability.\n\n**(c) Qualifying injury.** An avoidable injury that meets or exceeds the severity threshold in effect under \u00a7 10-2(c) on the date the injury occurred.\n\n**(d) Covered care.** Diagnosis, treatment, rehabilitation, developmental evaluation, special education, vocational training and placement, case management, counseling, behavioral and emotional therapy, residential and custodial care, attendant care, special equipment, home and vehicle modification, and related travel, in each case within a category specified under \u00a7 10-4(e).\n\n**(e) Employing entity.** The entity that bills for the health care in the course of which the injury arose. Where no entity bills other than an individual practitioner, that practitioner is the employing entity as to that care.\n\n**(f) Program.** The Patient Injury Compensation Program established by this Section.\n\n**(g) Fund.** The Patient Injury Compensation Fund established by \u00a7 10-15.\n\n**(h) Court.** The United States Patient Injury Court established by \u00a7 10-9.\n\n**(i) Panel.** A review panel constituted under \u00a7 10-4.\n\n**(j) Claimant.** The injured person, or the personal representative of an injured or deceased person.\n\n**(k) Covered civil action.** **A civil action, however denominated and whatever its source of law, the gravamen of which is that health care caused injury**, as \u00a7 10-3(a-1) through (a-3) provide. **The term is used throughout this Section and has the same meaning in each place.**"},{"anchor":"sec-10-2","heading":"\u00a7 10-2. Eligibility","part":null,"body":"**(a) Entitlement.** A claimant who has sustained a qualifying injury is entitled to compensation from the Fund as provided in \u00a7 10-6. This entitlement is a statutory right and is not conditioned on a finding of fault by any person.\n\n**(b) Causation.** Compensation is payable where the health care was a substantial factor in bringing about the injury. No other standard of causation applies. Neither the absence of an alternative explanation nor the presence of a contributing cause not attributable to health care defeats a claim, but compensation is reduced in proportion to any part of the injury that would have occurred without the health care.\n\n**(c) Severity threshold.** An injury meets the severity threshold if it caused incapacity for a continuous period of at least **the threshold period stated in the SCHEDULE**, or a permanent impairment of at least **the threshold impairment stated in the SCHEDULE** as assessed under the schedule published under \u00a7 10-6(e).\n\n**(d) Descent of the threshold.** The threshold shall decrease according to the schedule in the SCHEDULE to this Act **until it reaches the floor stated in the SCHEDULE, at which it remains.** **The threshold may not be increased.** No action of any officer, and no exercise of any authority however denominated, may raise the threshold above the level in effect on the day before that action. **Subject only to subsection (e), which permits a scheduled decrease to be deferred but never permits an increase.**\n\n**(d-1) The schedule of decreases.** The schedule shall be set so that each decrease is matched by adjudicative, panel and advocate capacity sufficient to meet the periods in \u00a7 10-10 at the resulting volume. **The floor and the terminal date are fixed in the SCHEDULE and are not committed to the discretion of any officer.**\n\n**(e) Pause.** The Court may, on its own motion and on a published finding that the number of matters pending under \u00a7 10-9 exceeds the capacity measure published under \u00a7 10-9(f), suspend a scheduled decrease for a period not exceeding **the pause period stated in the SCHEDULE**. **No suspension may be made where the excess is attributable in whole or in part to a failure to appoint special masters under \u00a7 10-9(b)(3).** A finding under this subsection shall state its reasons, shall be published, and is reviewable. A suspension does not reduce a threshold decrease that has already taken effect, and successive suspensions may not exceed **the maximum cumulative pause stated in the SCHEDULE** in the aggregate.\n\n**(f) Exclusion.** This Section does not apply to injury caused by a defect in a drug, biological product, or medical device, which remains subject to other law."},{"anchor":"sec-10-3","heading":"\u00a7 10-3. Filing required before civil action","part":null,"body":"**(a) Precondition.** No person may commence a covered civil action, in any court of the United States or of any State, unless a claim as to that injury has first been filed with the Program and one of the following has occurred: a determination has issued under \u00a7 10-5; the Program has certified under subsection (f) that the injury is not a qualifying injury; or the period in subsection (d) has elapsed.\n\n**(a-1) WHAT SUBSECTION (a) REACHES \u2014 the result, never the label.** Subsection (a) applies to **any civil action, however denominated and whatever its source of law, the gravamen of which is that health care caused injury** \u2014 including an action pleaded as, or seeking, any of the following:\n\n**(1)** negligence, professional negligence, or malpractice;\n\n**(2)** corporate, administrative, institutional, or systems negligence, and negligent credentialing, hiring, training, supervision, retention, or staffing;\n\n**(3)** premises liability, or liability for the condition or operation of a facility;\n\n**(4)** battery, assault, or want of informed consent;\n\n**(5)** fraud, constructive fraud, misrepresentation, concealment, or failure to disclose;\n\n**(6)** breach of contract, of warranty, or of fiduciary duty;\n\n**(7)** violation of a consumer protection, deceptive practices, or unfair trade practices statute of any State;\n\n**(8)** abuse, neglect, or exploitation under any State statute directed to patients, residents, elders, or vulnerable adults;\n\n**(9)** wrongful death, survival, loss of consortium, loss of services, or emotional distress suffered by a person who witnessed or learned of the injury;\n\n**(10)** spoliation or destruction of records; and\n\n**(11)** **PUNITIVE, EXEMPLARY, STATUTORY, MULTIPLIED, OR TREBLE DAMAGES, AND ATTORNEY FEES**, whether sought alone or with other relief.\n\n**(a-1A) The enumeration is illustrative.** **Paragraphs (1) through (11) of subsection (a-1) are examples and do not limit it.** **A claim not resembling any of them is within subsection (a) if its gravamen is that health care caused injury**, and no inference arises from the omission of any theory from that list.\n\n**(a-2) Closed on its face.** **A claim within subsection (a-1) is not removed from subsection (a) by** the label the pleading gives it \u00b7 the identity of the defendant \u00b7 that the defendant is an entity rather than a practitioner \u00b7 that the conduct alleged is administrative, corporate, or systemic rather than clinical \u00b7 that the claim is pleaded as intentional rather than negligent \u00b7 that the claim arises under a statute rather than at common law \u00b7 or that the relief sought is punitive or statutory rather than compensatory. **The exclusions are those and only those stated in subsection (a-3); the common law supplies none; and the omission of any other is a determination of Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(a-3) What is excluded.** Subsection (a) does not apply to \u2014\n\n**(1)** an action against a person other than a health care provider arising from a defect in a drug, biological product, or medical device, consistent with \u00a7 10-2(f);\n\n**(2)** an action seeking only injunctive or declaratory relief and no damages;\n\n**(3)** an action by the United States or a State, or an action brought on behalf of the United States or a State by a private person; or\n\n**(4)** an action for injury not alleged to have been caused by health care.\n\n**(a-4) Derivative and third-party claims.** **A person whose claim depends on an injury to another \u2014 including for wrongful death, survival, loss of consortium or services, or emotional distress from witnessing or learning of the injury \u2014 may not commence a civil action unless subsection (a) has been satisfied as to that injury.** **Commencement of such an action by any person has the effect stated in \u00a7 10-7(c) as to compensation payable to the injured person and to every person claiming through that injury.**\n\n**(b) Preservation of the right of action.** **This Section does not extinguish, abolish, or displace any right of action arising under the law of any State, and creates no defense to any such action.** A claimant who does not accept compensation under \u00a7 10-6 retains every right of action the claimant would have had if this Section had not been enacted. **What this Section does is (1) condition the time at which such an action may be commenced and (2) require, under \u00a7 10-7(c), that a claimant who commences such an action forgo compensation under this Section. It does nothing else to any right of action.**\n\n**(c) Tolling.** Every period of limitation applicable to an action described in subsection (a) is tolled from the date a claim is filed with the Program until the later of the date of a determination under \u00a7 10-5 or the close of the election period under \u00a7 10-7(a); and, where the claimant accepts compensation, for so long as any entitlement under \u00a7 10-6 remains payable.\n\n**(d) Outer bound.** Where the Program has not issued a determination within **the determination period stated in the SCHEDULE** of filing, the claimant may withdraw the claim and commence a civil action, and subsection (a) does not apply. Withdrawal under this subsection is not an election under \u00a7 10-7.\n\n**(e) Protective filings \u2014 stayed, never dismissed.** The waiver in \u00a7 10-7(c) does not attach to an action commenced before a determination is issued. **Such an action shall be stayed on the motion of any party, or on the court's own motion, until the determination issues or the period in subsection (d) elapses. It shall not be dismissed for want of compliance with subsection (a).**\n\n**(e-1) The precondition is not jurisdictional.** **Subsection (a) is a claim-processing rule.** **It does not limit the jurisdiction of any court, may not be raised by a court on its own motion, and is forfeited if not raised by a party before responding to the merits.**\n\n**(f) Certification of non-qualifying injury.** Within **the screening period stated in the SCHEDULE** of filing, the Program shall certify whether the injury, taken as alleged, could meet the severity threshold. **A certification that it could not satisfies subsection (a) immediately**, requires no panel, and is not a determination under \u00a7 10-5. A claimant may seek reconsideration of a certification under \u00a7 10-8, but need not do so before commencing a civil action."},{"anchor":"sec-10-4","heading":"\u00a7 10-4. Review panels","part":null,"body":"**(a) Constitution.** A review panel shall be constituted for each claim from a national roster maintained under subsection (c). **The Court shall maintain the roster, prescribe the method of assignment, and publish both. The Program shall perform the assignment as a ministerial act under the Court's published method, and shall publish an annual audit of its conformity.** **No officer or employee of the Program has discretion as to which panel members are assigned to a claim.**\n\n**(b) Composition.** Each panel shall consist of \u2014\n\n**(1)** at least one physician whose licensure, specialty, and practice setting match, as closely as available, those of the practitioner whose care is under review;\n\n**(2)** at least one physician who does not practice in that specialty; and\n\n**(3)** at least one member who is neither a health care practitioner nor employed by a health care provider, health plan, or entity that pays into the Fund.\n\n**(b-1) Every panel.** **The member described in subsection (b)(3) sits on every panel**, including every reconsideration panel constituted under \u00a7 10-8(b). **No determination under subsection (e) may be made by a panel not so constituted.**\n\n**(b-2) Function of the member under subsection (b)(3).** **The member's function is structural.** **A determination made only by physicians, as to physicians, carries a risk the peer-review literature documents and State medical boards illustrate; the member's presence is a check on that risk and is not an addition of clinical expertise.**\n\n**(b-3) Publication.** The Court shall publish annually, in the aggregate and by injury category, the proportion of panels reaching a unanimous determination and the proportion in which the member under subsection (b)(3) did not join the majority. **These figures shall not be published, compiled, or used by reference to any individual member.**\n\n**(c) Roster.** The Court shall maintain the roster, shall assign panel members at random subject to subsection (d), and shall publish annually the composition of the roster by specialty, practice setting, and category of member.\n\n**(c-1) Selection of members under subsection (b)(3).** Members under subsection (b)(3) shall be selected from among persons who apply, **stratified so that the roster as a whole corresponds, as nearly as practicable, to the population of the United States by age, sex, race and ethnicity, income, educational attainment, disability status, and rural or urban residence.** The Court shall publish annually the correspondence achieved and the respects in which it was not.\n\n**(c-2) Term; cap on service.** A member under subsection (b)(3) serves a term of **the community term stated in the SCHEDULE**, **is not eligible for reappointment**, and **may not sit on more than the number of panels stated in the SCHEDULE in a term.**\n\n**(c-3) Compensation \u2014 a floor and a ceiling.** A member under subsection (b)(3) shall be compensated, for each day of service, **the greater of** \u2014\n\n**(1)** a flat amount of **the per-diem floor stated in the SCHEDULE**; or\n\n**(2)** earnings actually lost by reason of the service, **not exceeding a daily maximum equal to the percentage stated in the SCHEDULE of the national average weekly wage divided by five.**\n\n**In addition**, a member shall be reimbursed expenses, travel, and the cost of care for a dependent or other person that is incurred to attend.\n\n**(c-3-1) Construction of the floor and the ceiling.** **A rate that does not replace lost earnings up to the maximum in (c-3)(2) is not sufficient for purposes of that paragraph**, and no lesser rate may be substituted by rule. **The floor in (c-3)(1) is payable to a member who has no lost earnings**, including a member who is retired, unemployed, or engaged in unpaid work in the home.\n\n**(c-4) Bounded preparation.** **A member under subsection (b)(3) shall be furnished a structured summary of the claim sufficient to perform the function described in subsection (b)(3), and is entitled to but not required to review the underlying record.** **No member is required to review the full record as a condition of service.**\n\n**(c-5) Protection of service.** **No employer may discharge, threaten, coerce, or penalize a person by reason of service or prospective service under subsection (b)(3), or of absence from employment for that service.** A person aggrieved may bring a civil action for reinstatement, lost wages and benefits, and reasonable attorney fees. **The remedies in this subsection are those and only those provided; no common-law rule supplies another, and the omission of any other remedy is a determination of Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(c-6) Compensation of physician members.** Members under subsection (b)(1) and (b)(2) shall be compensated at a rate sufficient to secure a roster representative of the field.\n\n**(c-7) Training and support.** **A member under subsection (b)(3) shall receive induction and training sufficient to perform the function in subsection (b)(3), furnished at no cost to the member and compensated under subsection (c-3) as service.** **The Court shall publish the curriculum.** **A member is entitled to a named point of contact for questions arising in the course of service.**\n\n**(d) Conflict screening.** No person may serve on a panel reviewing care delivered by a practitioner or at a facility with which that person has, or has had within **the conflict period stated in the SCHEDULE**, an employment, training, supervisory, referral, ownership, or competitive relationship, or any other relationship that would cause a reasonable person to question the member's impartiality.\n\n**(e) Determinations.** The panel shall determine \u2014 whether the injury was avoidable; the category of injury under the schedule published under \u00a7 10-6(e); whether the injury meets the severity threshold; whether causation is established under \u00a7 10-2(b); **and the categories of covered care attributable to the injury.**\n\n**(f) Analysis.** The panel shall prepare a written analysis of how the injury occurred and what would have avoided it. The analysis shall be published as provided in \u00a7 10-14.\n\n**(g-1) Where no matching physician is available.** Where no physician meeting subsection (b)(1) is available after reasonable effort, the Court may constitute the panel without that member, and shall record that it has done so."},{"anchor":"sec-10-5","heading":"\u00a7 10-5. Determination and payment","part":null,"body":"**(a) Ministerial payment.** Where the panel determines that an injury is a qualifying injury, the Fund shall pay compensation as provided in \u00a7 10-6. **No officer or employee of the United States has discretion to withhold, reduce, condition, or delay a payment required by this subsection.** The duty to pay is ministerial and is enforceable in mandamus.\n\n**(b) No discretion as to amount.** The amount payable is determined by application of the schedules published under \u00a7 10-6(e) to the panel's determinations. No person may set, negotiate, or adjust an amount so determined.\n\n**(c) Notice.** The Program shall furnish the claimant with the panel's determinations, the panel's analysis, the amount and form of compensation payable, a statement of the claimant's rights under \u00a7\u00a7 10-7 and 10-8, and notice that a de-identified record will be published under \u00a7 10-14.\n\n**(d) Denial.** Where the panel determines that a claim is not a qualifying injury, the Program shall furnish the claimant with the determination, the reasons for it, and notice of the right to reconsideration under \u00a7 10-8."},{"anchor":"sec-10-6","heading":"\u00a7 10-6. Compensation","part":null,"body":"**(a) Economic loss, paid as incurred.** The Fund shall pay the cost of covered care as it is incurred, for so long as it is incurred. **The Secretary may discharge this duty through the claims processing systems already used to administer title XVIII, and shall not construct a separate payment system where an existing one can be used.** **No lifetime limit applies to the cost of covered care.** The Fund shall not condition, reduce, or delay any payment on account of the availability of Medicaid, Medicare, or any other public program, and is the payer of first resort as to covered care.\n\n**(b) Lost earnings.** The Fund shall pay periodic compensation for earnings lost by reason of the injury, at **the replacement rate stated in the SCHEDULE** of pre-injury **after-tax** earnings, subject to **the periodic maximum stated in the SCHEDULE**, reassessed at intervals of not less than **the reassessment interval stated in the SCHEDULE**.\n\n**(b-1) After-tax earnings.** **Pre-injury after-tax earnings are pre-injury earnings reduced by the taxes that would have been imposed on them had they been earned**, being \u2014\n\n**(1)** the Federal income tax and the income taxes of the State and locality in which the injured person resided on the date of the injury; and\n\n**(2)** the tax imposed on the injured person by chapter 2, by section 3101, or by section 3201 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, as the case may be.\n\n**The taxes are computed as though those earnings were the only income of the injured person and of any spouse**, allowing the standard deduction and the credits for dependents available to the injured person, and **are not less than zero**. Filing status and dependents are those on the date of the injury or, at the claimant's election at any reassessment, those on the date of the reassessment.\n\n**(b-2) Ministerial computation.** **The Court shall publish annually, with the schedules under subsection (e), tables stating the taxes under subsection (b-1) for each State and locality, filing status, number of dependents and level of earnings, under the law in effect for the year of payment.** The Fund shall apply the tables, and no return of the claimant is required. **Where no table has been published for a year, the most recent table applies; where none has ever been published, or a table omits a State or locality, the income tax of that State or locality is taken as zero.**\n\n**(b-3) Credit for the payroll tax deducted.** **Where a payment under subsection (b) is reduced by a tax under subsection (b-1)(2), the injured person is credited with the earnings the payment replaces, and the amount of the tax is paid to the funds the tax would have financed.** In this subsection, **the earnings a payment replaces** are the pre-injury earnings by reference to which the payment is computed, before the reduction under subsection (b-1), multiplied by the percentage at which the SCHEDULE states the replacement rate.\n\n**(1) Social Security.** Where the tax is under chapter 2 or section 3101 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, then, for purposes of determining entitlement to and the amount of any monthly benefit or lump-sum death payment payable under title II of the Social Security Act on the basis of the wages and self-employment income of the injured person, and for purposes of section 216(i)(3) of that Act, **the injured person shall be deemed to have been paid as wages, in each calendar year, the earnings replaced by payments under subsection (b) made for any period in that year, whenever made**, not exceeding, together with the injured person's other wages and self-employment income for that year, the contribution and benefit base determined under section 230 of that Act. **Amounts so deemed are not wages or earnings for purposes of section 203 of that Act, and are not evidence of the ability to engage in substantial gainful activity for purposes of section 222(c) or 223(d)(4) of that Act.**\n\n**(2) Railroad retirement.** Where the tax is under section 3201 of that Code, **the earnings the payments replace are deemed remuneration paid to the injured person for time lost as an employee**, by the employer that employed the injured person on the date of the injury, **within the meaning of section 1(h) of the Railroad Retirement Act of 1974** (45 U.S.C. 231(h)), deemed earned in the months for which the payments are made, and each such month is a month in which the injured person received remuneration for time lost for purposes of section 1(f)(1) of that Act. Where both this paragraph and paragraph (1) apply, each reaches only the earnings on which its own tax was computed. **No tax is imposed on any employer, employee or other person by reason of this paragraph.**\n\n**(3) Payment to the funds.** **The Fund shall pay an amount equal to the tax by which payments under subsection (b) were reduced** \u2014\n\n**(A)** as to tax under section 1401(a) or 3101(a) of that Code, to the Federal Old-Age and Survivors Insurance Trust Fund and the Federal Disability Insurance Trust Fund, in the proportions in which section 201 of the Social Security Act would appropriate amounts equivalent to that tax to each;\n\n**(B)** as to tax under section 1401(b) or 3101(b) of that Code, to the Federal Hospital Insurance Trust Fund;\n\n**(C)** as to tax under section 3201(a) of that Code, to the Social Security Equivalent Benefit Account established by section 15A of the Railroad Retirement Act of 1974 (45 U.S.C. 231n-1); and\n\n**(D)** as to tax under section 3201(b) of that Code, to the Railroad Retirement Account maintained under section 15(a) of that Act (45 U.S.C. 231n(a)).\n\n**(4) Ministerial reporting and crediting.** **Not later than the last day of January of each year, the Fund shall report to the Commissioner of Social Security or to the Railroad Retirement Board, as the case may be, the amounts deemed under paragraph (1) or (2) for each injured person by reason of payments made in the preceding calendar year, stating the year or months to which each amount is attributed, and shall make the payments under paragraph (3) for that year.** **The Commissioner and the Board shall credit the amounts reported to the injured person's record.** Each duty in this paragraph is ministerial and is enforceable in mandamus by the claimant.\n\n**(5) No tax.** **Nothing in this subsection makes any amount wages, compensation, or self-employment income for purposes of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986**, and \u00a7 10-17A applies to every payment under this section, except that \u00a7 10-17A(b) does not limit paragraph (1) or (2).\n\n**(c) Non-economic compensation.** The Fund shall pay, in a single payment within **the payment period stated in the SCHEDULE** of the determination, an amount fixed by the schedule published under subsection (e) according to the category and severity of the injury. **That amount does not vary with the claimant's earnings, occupation, age, or dependents.**\n\n**(d) Presumption within a specified category.** Covered care within a category specified under \u00a7 10-4(e) is presumed attributable to the injury. The presumption may be rebutted only by a showing that the particular care was neither caused by nor reasonably related to the injury.\n\n**(e) Schedules.** The Court shall publish, and revise not more often than **the revision interval stated in the SCHEDULE**, a schedule of injury categories, a schedule of severity, and a schedule of non-economic amounts. A revision does not reduce an amount payable on a claim already determined.\n\n**(e-1) How the non-economic schedule is set.** For each injury category, the amount shall be set by reference to **the value a claimant in that category could expect to realize from a civil action**, which is the award such a claimant could expect to recover **reduced by** \u2014\n\n**(1)** the probability that no recovery would be obtained at all;\n\n**(2)** the proportion of any recovery payable as attorney fees, no part of which is deducted from compensation under this Section;\n\n**(3)** the delay before a recovery would be received, including the prospect of appeal; and\n\n**(4)** the burden of litigation itself.\n\n**(e-2) Construction of subsection (e-1).** **Each reduction in subsection (e-1) reflects a benefit this Section confers rather than a discount taken from the claimant**, and the amount set is not required to equal, and shall not be set at, the award a claimant might recover in a civil action. **Paragraph (4) is not susceptible of measurement and shall not be assigned a numerical value; it is a reason the amount may be set below the figure produced by paragraphs (1) through (3), and is not a reason to set it lower than that figure by any stated proportion.**\n\n**(e-3) Sources, at the initial setting.** In setting the schedule for the first time, the Court shall derive the expected award under subsection (e-1) from **records of amounts actually paid**, and shall state its sources. **The Court shall not derive it from reported verdicts alone**. **The Court shall not derive it from amounts paid under this Section.**\n\n**(e-4) Revision \u2014 the schedule is INDEXED, and is not re-derived from civil recoveries.** After the initial setting, each amount shall be adjusted, with effect on each anniversary of the initial setting, by multiplying the amount then in effect by one plus **the general earnings measure stated in the SCHEDULE**, expressed as a decimal fraction, and by no other method. **The Court shall not revise an amount by reference to amounts recovered in civil actions, whether by verdict, settlement, or report of payment.**\n\n**(e-6) One-way.** No revision may reduce an amount below the amount previously in effect.\n\n**(e-7) Uniformity.** The schedule is national. **The amount for an injury category does not vary with the State in which the care was delivered, or with whether that State limits damages in a civil action.**\n\n**(f) Vesting.** An entitlement that has arisen under this Section continues according to its terms notwithstanding the repeal or amendment of this Section, and notwithstanding any lapse in appropriations.\n\n**(g) Failure to pay.** Where the Fund fails to pay an amount due under this Section and the failure is not cured within **the cure period stated in the SCHEDULE** of written notice, the claimant may \u2014 at the claimant's election \u2014 proceed under \u00a7 10-9(c)(4), or commence a civil action as though the claimant had made no election under \u00a7 10-7, in which case amounts already paid are credited against any recovery.\n\n**(h) Compensation is not assignable.** \u2014 An entitlement under this Section may not be sold, assigned, or transferred before payment, and any purported assignment is void."},{"anchor":"sec-10-7","heading":"\u00a7 10-7. Election and waiver","part":null,"body":"**(a) Election.** Within **the election period stated in the SCHEDULE** of receiving notice under \u00a7 10-5(c), a claimant shall accept the compensation determined or decline it. A claimant who declines may proceed under \u00a7 10-8 or \u00a7 10-9, or may commence a civil action.\n\n**(b) Acceptance.** Acceptance entitles the claimant to compensation under \u00a7 10-6 and does not require the claimant to release any person from any obligation other than as provided in subsection (c).\n\n**(c) Waiver.** **Commencement by a claimant of a civil action for damages arising from the injury operates as an irrevocable waiver of all compensation under this Section, whether already determined or arising thereafter.** This subsection does not apply to \u2014\n\n**(1)** an action commenced under \u00a7 10-6(g) by reason of the Fund's failure to pay;\n\n**(2)** an action commenced after withdrawal under \u00a7 10-3(d); or\n\n**(3)** an action stayed under \u00a7 10-3(e).\n\n**(c-1) Repayment where compensation has already been received.** **Where a claimant has received any amount under \u00a7 10-6 and thereafter commences a civil action within \u00a7 10-3(a), the amounts received are a first lien on any recovery in that action and shall be repaid to the Fund out of it.** **The lien attaches before any attorney fee, cost, or other charge against the recovery.**\n\n**(c-2) No recovery twice by another route.** **Where any person recovers in a civil action within \u00a7 10-3(a) on account of an injury, no compensation is payable under this Section to any person on account of that injury**, and amounts already paid are subject to subsection (c-1).\n\n**(d) No contingent fee on program compensation.** No agreement may provide for the payment to any person of a fee measured as a proportion of compensation payable under \u00a7 10-6. An agreement in violation of this subsection is void, and no obligation arises under it."},{"anchor":"sec-10-8","heading":"\u00a7 10-8. Reconsideration","part":null,"body":"**(a) Right.** A claimant may obtain reconsideration of any determination under \u00a7 10-4(e), of a determination that covered care falls outside a specified category, or of a denial under \u00a7 10-5(d).\n\n**(b) Reviewers.** Reconsideration shall be conducted by a panel none of whose members participated in the determination under review.\n\n**(c) Record.** Reconsideration shall be on the written record. No hearing is required and none may be demanded.\n\n**(d) Cost.** No fee may be charged to a claimant for reconsideration.\n\n**(e) Exhaustion.** No matter within subsection (a) may be brought under \u00a7 10-9 unless reconsideration has been sought and completed, or the period in \u00a7 10-10(a)(2) has elapsed."},{"anchor":"sec-10-9","heading":"\u00a7 10-9. The United States Patient Injury Court","part":null,"body":"**(a) Establishment.** There is established a court of record under Article I of the Constitution, to be known as the United States Patient Injury Court.\n\n**(b) Judges; special masters.**\n\n**(1)** The Court shall consist of **the number of judges stated in the SCHEDULE**, appointed by the President by and with the advice and consent of the Senate for **the judicial term stated in the SCHEDULE**, removable by the President only for inefficiency, neglect of duty, or malfeasance in office, after notice and opportunity for a hearing.\n\n**(2)** The judges of the Court shall appoint special masters by vote of a majority of the judges in regular active service, for **the master term stated in the SCHEDULE**. **A special master may be removed only by the judges of the Court, and only for incompetency, misconduct, neglect of duty, disability, or other good cause shown.** No officer of the United States other than a judge of the Court has any authority over the appointment, tenure, compensation, assignment, or removal of a special master.\n\n**(3)** The number of special masters shall be not fewer than the number necessary to satisfy the deadlines in \u00a7 10-10. **Where the measure published under subsection (f) shows that pending matters exceed capacity, or that the times published under that subsection exceed the deadlines in \u00a7 10-10, the Court shall appoint additional special masters and **shall report to the Congress the number of special masters, panel members, claimant advocates, interpreters and clerks required to meet those deadlines at the resulting volume**, renewed annually until they are met, within the reporting period stated in the SCHEDULE.** A failure to appoint does not extend any period under \u00a7 10-10 or affect the consequence in \u00a7 10-10(b).\n\n**(c) Jurisdiction.** A special master shall hear and determine, and no other matter arising under this Section may be brought before the Court \u2014\n\n**(1)** a claimant's challenge to a determination under \u00a7 10-4(e), following reconsideration;\n\n**(2)** a question of law arising under this Section;\n\n**(3)** the termination or reduction of an entitlement under \u00a7 10-6, but not the denial of a particular item of covered care;\n\n**(4)** an action under \u00a7 10-6(g); and\n\n**(5)** a challenge by an employing entity under \u00a7 10-16(e).\n\n**(d) Scope of review under (c)(1).** On a challenge under subsection (c)(1) the special master shall determine avoidability, causation, the category of injury, and whether the severity threshold is met. **The special master shall not determine, and has no authority to determine, the amount payable within a category.** Nothing in this subsection permits an award other than as the schedules under \u00a7 10-6(e) provide.\n\n**(e) Medical judgment.** **The special master shall determine de novo whether the injury was avoidable, whether causation is established, the category of injury, and whether the severity threshold is met.** In doing so the special master shall accept the panel's assessment of **what the highest standard of care and expertise required in the circumstances** unless no reasonable panel could have so assessed it. **The content of the standard is reviewed deferentially; its application to the injury is not.**\n\n**(f) Capacity measure.** The Court shall publish annually a measure of the number of matters its special masters can determine within the deadlines in \u00a7 10-10, and the number pending. **The publication states, in addition: the time from filing to determination at the median and at the ninetieth percentile, by tier; the number of panels that could not be constituted and the reason; and the number of claimants for whom no advocate was available under \u00a7 10-11.**\n\n**(g) Publication.** Every determination of a special master shall be published, with identifying information removed as provided in \u00a7 10-14."},{"anchor":"sec-10-10","heading":"\u00a7 10-10. Deadlines","part":null,"body":"**(a) Periods.**\n\n**(1)** The Program shall issue a determination under \u00a7 10-5 within **the determination period stated in the SCHEDULE** of filing.\n\n**(2)** Reconsideration under \u00a7 10-8 shall be completed within **the reconsideration period stated in the SCHEDULE** of the request.\n\n**(3)** A special master shall determine a matter within **the master period stated in the SCHEDULE** of its filing.\n\n**(b) Consequence of failure to decide.** **Where a period in subsection (a) elapses without a determination, the claimant's position prevails.** Specifically \u2014\n\n**(1)** on failure under (a)(1), the claim is allowed as filed;\n\n**(2)** on failure under (a)(2), the determination sought on reconsideration is entered; and\n\n**(3)** on failure under (a)(3), the classification asserted by the claimant is adopted.\n\n**(c) No extension.** A period in subsection (a) may not be extended by consent, by rule, or by any officer. The consequence in subsection (b) operates without any person applying for it and may not be waived by the Program, the Fund, or the Court.\n\n**(d) Construction.** Subsection (b) does not authorize a payment exceeding the schedules published under \u00a7 10-6(e).\n\n**(e) Minority and incapacity.** \u2014 A period under this Section does not run against an individual while the individual is under 18 years of age or is incapacitated, and begins to run when that condition ends."},{"anchor":"sec-10-11","heading":"\u00a7 10-11. Representation","part":null,"body":"**(a) Three functions, separately provided.** Assistance to claimants comprises three functions, which differ in volume and in the skill they require, and which shall not be provided by a single body \u2014\n\n**(1) take-up and navigation** \u2014 informing people that this Section exists, how to file, and what a determination means, including advice at the election under \u00a7 10-7(a);\n\n**(2) representation** in contested matters under \u00a7 10-8; and\n\n**(3) counsel** in matters under \u00a7 10-9, provided through subsection (d).\n\n**(a-1) Function (1) \u2014 accredited organizations.** The Court shall accredit organizations and individuals to perform the function in subsection (a)(1) and shall fund them from the account described in \u00a7 10-15(b)(1). **No organization may be accredited that receives funding, directly or indirectly, from a person assessed under \u00a7 10-15(d) or (e)**, and accreditation shall be withdrawn on such funding being received.\n**The Court shall also provide the information and decision support described in subsection (a)(1) by means that do not require a person to be assigned to each claim**, and shall report annually on the proportion of claimants assisted by each means.\n\n**(a-2) Function (2) \u2014 an independent office.** There is established, independent of the Fund and of the Program, an Office of Patient Injury Advocates, which shall furnish representation without charge in proceedings under \u00a7 10-8. **The Office shall be funded by a dedicated line within the account described in \u00a7 10-15(b)(1), which may not be reduced by the Program, the Fund, or any officer administering either.**\n\n**(b) Independence.** No officer or employee of the Fund or of the Program may appoint, direct, supervise, evaluate, or remove an advocate or an accredited organization, or review advice given to a claimant. **Independence from the Fund and Program is required of both functions; independence from the entities that finance the Fund is required additionally of accredited organizations under subsection (a-1).**\n\n**(c) Compensation.** An advocate, and an accredited organization, shall be compensated at a fixed rate that does not vary with \u2014 whether a claimant accepts or declines compensation, the amount of compensation determined, or whether a matter proceeds under \u00a7 10-8 or \u00a7 10-9.\n\n**(c-1) And they may not be EVALUATED on it either.** \u2014 No person may measure, rank, promote, retain, or evaluate an advocate or an accredited organization by reference to whether claimants accept or decline compensation, by the amounts determined, or by the proportion of matters that proceed under \u00a7 10-8 or \u00a7 10-9.\n\n**(c-2) What accreditation requires.** \u2014 An accredited organization or individual shall complete training prescribed by the Court, shall certify annually that it remains in compliance with this Section, and is subject to standards of conduct prescribed by the Court. **Accreditation may be withdrawn for a violation of those standards.**\n\n**(c-3) Advice at the election is recorded.** \u2014 Where advice is given under subsection (a)(1) in connection with an election under \u00a7 10-7, the accredited organization shall furnish the claimant a brief written statement of the advice given.\n\n**(d) Attorney fees.** The Court shall award reasonable attorney fees and costs to a claimant represented by counsel in a proceeding under \u00a7 10-9, whether or not the claimant prevails, if the matter was brought in good faith and upon a reasonable basis.\n\n**(e) Determination of fees.** Fees under subsection (d) shall be determined by the special master. **Neither the Fund nor the Program may determine, object to the reasonableness of, or condition the payment of a fee under this subsection.**\n\n**(f) Paid in addition.** An award under subsection (d), the cost of accreditation and funding under subsection (a-1), and the cost of the Office established by subsection (a-2), shall be paid from the account described in \u00a7 10-15(b)(1) **in addition to, and never by deduction from, compensation payable to a claimant.**"},{"anchor":"sec-10-11a","heading":"\u00a7 10-11A. Notice by health care providers","part":null,"body":"**(a) Duty.** A health care provider shall furnish to each patient a written notice describing this Section, what it compensates, that no fault need be shown, that filing is free, that assistance is available under \u00a7 10-11(a-1), and how to file.\n\n**(b) When furnished.** The notice shall be furnished \u2014\n\n**(1)** **not later than the intake notice period stated in the SCHEDULE after** the commencement of a course of care, or after registration or admission;\n\n**(2)** **on the occurrence of a condition identified as not present on admission under section 1886(d)(4)(D) of the Social Security Act, or of an event the provider is required to report to any State or accrediting body as a serious reportable event** \u2014 in either case within **the triggered notice period stated in the SCHEDULE** of the coding or report; and\n\n**(3)** on the request of a patient or the patient's representative, at any time.\n\n**(b-0) Each notice is separately required.** **Furnishing a notice under one paragraph of subsection (b) does not satisfy any other paragraph**, and subsection (e) operates on the failure to furnish any notice that was required. **A notice under paragraph (2) need not be furnished where a notice under paragraph (1) was furnished within the triggered notice period stated in the SCHEDULE before the coding or report.**\n\n**(b-0A) WHEN THE PATIENT HAS ALREADY GONE.** Where the duty arises after the patient has left, the notice shall be furnished to the last address the provider holds for the patient and, where the patient uses one, through the provider's patient portal. **The record required by subsection (d) is the record of transmission**, and a notice returned undelivered does not satisfy the duty.\n\n**(b-1) Coding, and the reportable-event determination.** **A determination that a condition was present on admission, made or altered for the purpose of avoiding a duty under subsection (b)(2), is a false statement material to a claim for payment**, as it already is where it affects payment under section 1886(d)(4)(D) of the Social Security Act. **A determination that an event is not a serious reportable event, made or altered for the purpose of avoiding a duty under subsection (b)(2), is subject to this subsection on the same terms.**\n\n**(c) Form.** The notice shall be a separate document, not combined with a consent, a privacy notice, a financial agreement, or any other instrument, and shall be written in plain language. **It shall be furnished in the languages in which the provider is already required to furnish notices or taglines under section 1557 of the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act and the regulations under it, and in no fewer.**\n\n**(d) No acknowledgment required.** **A provider shall not condition care, registration, or admission on a patient signing or acknowledging the notice, and no signature or acknowledgment is required.** **The provider shall record that the notice was furnished, and shall retain the record.**\n\n**(d-1) What satisfies the record.** **The record required by subsection (d) may be satisfied, as to notice under subsection (b)(1), by evidence that the provider's registration or admission process furnishes the notice to every patient as a matter of course**, together with the provider's attestation to that effect, renewed annually. **A record for each individual patient is not required.** **Notice under subsection (b)(2) shall be recorded individually.**\n\n**(e) Consequence of failure \u2014 self-executing.** **Where a provider has not furnished a notice required by any paragraph of subsection (b) and cannot produce the record required by subsection (d), no period of limitation under this Section, and no period of limitation applicable to a civil action arising from the injury, runs against that patient until the notice is furnished.** **This subsection operates without any determination by any officer, requires no finding of intent, and confers no other remedy. The absence of the record is what has effect; nothing turns on why it is absent.**\n\n**(e-1) When the toll ends.** A period tolled under subsection (e) begins to run on the earlier of \u2014\n\n**(1)** the date the notice is furnished; or\n\n**(2)** the date the patient acquired actual knowledge \u2014 **of the existence of this Section, where the failure was of a notice under subsection (b)(1); or of both the existence of this Section and the injury, where the failure was of a notice under subsection (b)(2).**\n\n**(e-2) Actual knowledge, and only actual knowledge.** **Knowledge under subsection (e-1)(2) means the patient in fact knew.** **It is not established by** what the patient could have discovered, should have known, or was on notice to inquire into \u00b7 the patient's failure to inquire \u00b7 the public availability of the notice published under subsection (f) or of any other information \u00b7 the patient's general awareness that claims for medical injury exist \u00b7 or the patient's having been represented by counsel for any other purpose. **The exclusions are those and only those stated; the common law supplies no further route to knowledge; and the omission of any other is a determination of Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(e-3) Burden.** **The provider bears the burden of establishing knowledge under subsection (e-1)(2), by clear and convincing evidence.** No inference arises from the passage of time.\n\n**(e-4) Minimum period after the toll ends.** **Where a period begins to run under subsection (e-1), the patient has not less than the post-knowledge period stated in the SCHEDULE in which to act, notwithstanding that the period would otherwise have expired.**\n\n**(e-5) FURNISHING A NOTICE IS NOT AN ADMISSION.** **A notice under this section, the fact that it was furnished, and the record kept under subsection (d), are admissible in any proceeding only to prove whether the notice was furnished, and for no other purpose.** **They are not evidence that an injury occurred, that an injury was avoidable, that care departed from any standard, or that the provider believed any of those things.** **\u00a7 10-12(d) does not make a notice or its record admissible for any other purpose, and this subsection governs over it.**\n\n**(f) Publication.** The Court shall publish a model notice, which a provider may use, and use of which satisfies subsection (c).\n\n**(g) Reach.** This Section applies to a health care provider engaged in commerce or in an activity affecting commerce. **Where a provider is not so engaged, the duty in subsection (a) applies as a condition of participation in any program under title XVIII or title XIX of the Social Security Act.**"},{"anchor":"sec-10-12","heading":"\u00a7 10-12. Inadmissibility","part":null,"body":"**(a) Bar.** **No court of the United States and no court of any State may admit, receive, or permit any use of** a determination of a panel under \u00a7 10-4(e), a panel's analysis under \u00a7 10-4(f), a determination of a special master, or a record generated by the Program, **in any covered civil action, or in any other civil action arising from health care** \u2014 including for impeachment, for refreshing recollection, as a foundation for expert opinion, or for any other purpose.\n\n**(a-1) An entity's self-report is not an admission, and is not discoverable.** **A report made to the Program under \u00a7 10-16(d), and the fact that it was made, are not an admission of fault or of liability, and are not evidence that any person was negligent, that care departed from any standard of care, or that the entity or any practitioner believed either.** **Subsection (a) applies to the report and to the fact that it was made as it applies to a record generated by the Program.** **The report may be received only in a proceeding under this Section, or in a prosecution or an action by or on behalf of the United States in which the falsity of the report is in issue, and for no other purpose.** **In any other civil action in any court of the United States or of any State, the report and the fact that it was made are not subject to discovery, and no person may be compelled in discovery to produce the report or to state whether it was made or what it contains.** **Subsection (d) applies: the facts the report states may be proved by the medical record and by any other evidence that would be admissible had no report been made.**\n\n**(b) Reciprocal.** No determination, finding, verdict, or judgment in a civil action may be received in a proceeding under this Section as evidence of whether an injury was avoidable.\n\n**(c) The reason, stated operatively.** Avoidability under \u00a7 10-1(a) and negligence are distinct questions governed by distinct standards. A determination under this Section is not evidence of negligence and is not probative of it.\n\n**(d) What is not privileged.** **This Section does not render inadmissible, privileged, or undiscoverable** the medical record, billing or discharge information, or any other original patient or provider record, or any fact that would be admissible had no claim been filed. This Section confers no privilege on information by reason only of its having been furnished to the Program; **subsection (a-1) bars the use and the discovery of a report under \u00a7 10-16(d) itself, and not of the facts it states.**\n\n**(d-1) Publication is not waiver.** **Publication under \u00a7 10-14 does not waive subsection (a) and does not make a published record admissible.** A record published under \u00a7 10-14 remains subject to subsection (a) in every forum.\n\n**(e) Clear statement.** **The presumption against the preemption of State rules of evidence, privilege, and procedure is expressly displaced as to subsections (a) and (a-1).** Congress intends subsections (a) and (a-1) to govern in every proceeding in every forum. **The limits on subsection (a) are those and only those stated in subsections (a-1), (b), (c) and (d); the common law supplies none; and the omission of any other exception is a determination of Congress and not a gap.** **Subsection (a) rests on the authority of Congress to regulate commerce among the several States, health care being an economic activity conducted across State lines, and on the same authority sustaining section 407 of title 23, United States Code (formerly section 409 of that title).** **Subsection (a-1) rests on the same authority.**"},{"anchor":"sec-10-13","heading":"\u00a7 10-13. Reporting","part":null,"body":"**(a) Data Bank.** **A payment made by the Fund under \u00a7 10-6 is not a payment made by an entity in settlement or satisfaction of a claim on behalf of a physician, dentist, or other health care practitioner**, and is not reportable under section 401 of the Health Care Quality Improvement Act of 1986.\n\n**(b) State reporting.** No State licensing, registration, or reporting requirement may treat a payment made by the Fund, a determination under \u00a7 10-4(e), or participation in the Program as a payment made on behalf of a practitioner. **Nor may any such determination or payment be received as evidence of professional misconduct in a disciplinary proceeding.** **This subsection limits what may be REPORTED and what may be RECEIVED IN EVIDENCE. It does not limit what a board may investigate or find on evidence obtained independently of the Program.**\n\n**(c) What is preserved.** **This Section does not limit the authority of any licensing board to receive a complaint, to investigate, or to discipline**, and does not limit any reporting obligation arising from a fact independently known. A patient may make a complaint to any licensing board without effect on any right under this Section, and the making or outcome of such a complaint is not a ground for withholding, reducing, or delaying compensation."},{"anchor":"sec-10-14","heading":"\u00a7 10-14. Publication","part":null,"body":"**(a) Duty.** For each claim on which compensation is paid, the Program shall publish a record of the injury and the panel's analysis under \u00a7 10-4(f). Publication is ministerial and is enforceable in mandamus.\n\n**(b) De-identification.** A record published under this Section shall be de-identified as to the patient **and as to the practitioner**, by the expert-determination method under section 164.514(b)(1) of title 45, Code of Federal Regulations. **The determination may be made as to a method applied to a class of records rather than record by record, provided the documentation that section requires is prepared and published for the method and reviewed at intervals of not more than the methodology review interval stated in the SCHEDULE.** Institutions may be identified only in aggregate counts published under subsection (e).\n\n**(c) Suppression.** The Program may withhold a record only on a written finding, published in redacted form, that de-identification satisfying subsection (b) **cannot be achieved**. That a record would embarrass or disadvantage any person is not a ground.\n\n**(d) Claimant's rights.** A claimant shall be notified at filing that a record will be published, and may seek review under \u00a7 10-8 on the ground that subsection (b) is not satisfied. **There is no right to withhold publication on any other ground.**\n\n**(e) Aggregate data.** The Program shall publish annually, by injury category \u2014 the number of claims filed, determined, allowed, and denied; **the number and proportion of claimants who declined compensation under \u00a7 10-7**; the number of matters under \u00a7\u00a7 10-8 and 10-9 and their outcomes; amounts paid; and counts by institution."},{"anchor":"sec-10-15","heading":"\u00a7 10-15. Funding","part":null,"body":"**(a) Fund.** There is established in the Treasury the Patient Injury Compensation Fund, available without fiscal year limitation and without further appropriation for the purposes of this Section. Amounts in the Fund shall not be transferred or obligated for any other purpose.\n\n**(b) Sources.** The Fund consists of \u2014\n\n**(1)** amounts appropriated under subsection (c), which shall bear the cost of the Court, the Program, the Office established by \u00a7 10-11(a), and awards under \u00a7 10-11(d);\n\n**(2)** the provider assessment under \u00a7 10-15(d);\n\n**(3)** the health plan assessment under \u00a7 10-15(e); and\n\n**(4)** amounts credited under \u00a7 10-17(b).\n\n**(c) Appropriation.** There is appropriated to the Fund, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, such sums as are necessary to meet the obligations of this Section that are not met by the sources in subsection (b)(2) through (4). This appropriation is permanent and is available without fiscal year limitation. **It does not meet an obligation to the extent the shortfall is attributable to a failure to assess under subsection (d) or (e).** **The Treasury does not relieve an assessed entity of a charge the Secretary declined to impose.**\n\n**(d) Provider assessment.** The Secretary shall assess entities that bill for health care, allocated by sector and, within a sector, by specialty risk class and revenue. **The aggregate of assessments under this subsection in any year shall not exceed the aggregate amount expended on medical professional liability insurance premiums and self-insurance in the base period specified in the SCHEDULE**, that amount being adjusted, with effect on each anniversary of the date of enactment, by multiplying the amount then in effect by one plus **the general earnings measure stated in the SCHEDULE**, expressed as a decimal fraction. **The aggregate limit is a ceiling on the total assessed, and each entity's share is continuous beneath it \u2014 no entity's assessment steps at the limit.**\n\n**(1) Attribution.** Liability is attributed to the sector that billed for the care in the course of which the injury arose, as identified in the panel's analysis under \u00a7 10-4(f).\n\n**(2) Specialty risk class.** Risk classes shall be those maintained by medical professional liability insurers as of the date of enactment, and shall be replaced, on the schedule in the SCHEDULE, by classes derived from determinations under \u00a7 10-4(e). **A risk class may not be derived from amounts paid.**\n\n**(2-A) Adjustment for differential claiming.** **Determinations under \u00a7 10-4(e) arise only where a claim is filed, and patients do not file at the same rate across specialties.** **A class derived from those determinations without adjustment would rate a specialty as safer by reason of its patients being less likely to claim.** **The Secretary shall therefore publish, by specialty, the rate at which injuries are claimed, and shall adjust risk classes for differences in that rate.** **A methodology that does not so adjust may not be used.**\n\n**(3)** No assessment is imposed on an individual practitioner other than as an employing entity.\n\n**(4) Continuation by operation of law.** Where the Secretary has not made an assessment under this subsection for a year, **the most recent assessment continues in force for that year by operation of this paragraph**, each entity's amount for that year being its amount for the preceding year multiplied by one plus **the general earnings measure stated in the SCHEDULE**, expressed as a decimal fraction, and reduced pro rata to the extent the aggregate would otherwise exceed the limit in this subsection, and is due and collectible as if newly assessed. Where no assessment has yet been made, **the aggregate permitted by this subsection is assessed pro rata by sector and revenue on the most recent data available to the Secretary.** **No further act by any person is required, and the absence of a risk class, a published claiming rate, or a methodology does not suspend this paragraph.**\n\n**(e) Health plan assessment.** The Secretary shall assess health plans **on the basis stated in the SCHEDULE**.\n\n**(f) Surplus.** Amounts in the Fund exceeding the reserve specified in the SCHEDULE shall be applied to research and programming in patient safety, with preference to research using records published under \u00a7 10-14. **No amount may be returned, credited, or rebated to any entity assessed under subsection (d) or (e).** No grant may be conditioned on the approval of the Fund, the Program, or any person assessed, and publication of results may not be so conditioned."},{"anchor":"sec-10-16","heading":"\u00a7 10-16. Alignment charge","part":null,"body":"**(a) Charge.** An employing entity shall pay to the Fund **the coinsurance rate stated in the SCHEDULE** of each amount paid under \u00a7 10-6 on a claim arising from care for which that entity billed.\n\n**(b) Annual limit.** The aggregate of charges under subsection (a) on any entity in a year shall not exceed **the aggregate cap stated in the SCHEDULE**, which shall be lower, as a proportion of the entity's assessment under \u00a7 10-15(d), for entities of smaller assessment.\n\n**(c) Neutrality.** The charge is the same whether the claim was determined under \u00a7 10-5, on reconsideration, or by a special master, and does not vary with any position taken by the entity.\n\n**(d) Self-identification.** **No charge under subsection (a) arises on a claim where the employing entity reported the injury to the Program before the claim was filed, in a report that** \u2014\n\n**(1)** names the patient;\n\n**(2)** states the date of the event in the course of which the injury arose, describes the injury that occurred, and identifies the entry, if any, described in paragraph (3);\n\n**(3)** was filed within **the self-report period stated in the SCHEDULE** after **the date of the first entry, in any record kept by or for the employing entity, that records the injury**, that date being **the date on which the entry was made, as shown by the record and by any audit trail kept of it**; or, where no record kept by or for the employing entity recorded the injury before the report was filed, at any time before the claim was filed; and\n\n**(4)** describes the injury determined on the claim under \u00a7 10-4(e), arising from the event on the date stated, or an injury of which the injury so determined is a consequence.\n\n**(d-1) What a report is not.** **A report naming more than one patient, describing an injury by reference to a class of patients or of care, or describing a risk of injury or an injury that may occur rather than one that has occurred, does not satisfy subsection (d) as to any patient.** **Whether a report satisfies subsection (d) is determined by the Program on the face of the report, the panel's determinations under \u00a7 10-4(e) and, as to subsection (d)(3), the records before the panel, and is not affected by any other report the entity filed.** **The date governing subsection (d)(3) is that of the first entry recording the injury in fact, whether or not the report identifies it.**\n\n**(d-2) Not an admission.** **A report under subsection (d) is not an admission of fault and may not be used in, or obtained by discovery in, a civil action, as \u00a7 10-12(a-1) provides.**\n\n**(e) Challenge; payment unaffected.** An entity may challenge the attribution of a claim to it, or a determination that a report does not satisfy subsection (d), under \u00a7 10-9(c)(5). **Compensation to the claimant shall be paid in full when due without regard to any such challenge, and a claimant is not a party to it.**\n\n**(f) Security.** The Secretary may require security proportionate to expected liability from an entity that does not meet the financial capacity standard applied under Component 6.\n\n**(g) Credit.** Amounts collected under this Section shall be credited against the paying entity's assessment under \u00a7 10-15(d), and any remainder applied under \u00a7 10-15(f). **Amounts collected under this Section may not be treated as a source of funds for any purpose of this Section, and no estimate of receipts under this Section may be taken into account in setting an assessment under \u00a7 10-15(d) or (e).**"},{"anchor":"sec-10-17","heading":"\u00a7 10-17. Repeal and redirection","part":null,"body":"**(a) Repeal \u2014 effected elsewhere.** Section 1886(p) of the Social Security Act (the hospital-acquired condition reduction program) is repealed by section 6-13(b), together with the hospital inpatient quality reporting program, the hospital readmissions reduction program and the hospital value-based purchasing program. **This Section effects no repeal and depends on none: subsection (b) operates on the amount those programs withheld, whether or not section 6-13 is enacted.**\n\n**(b) Redirection.** **There shall be transferred to the Fund, from the Federal Hospital Insurance Trust Fund, the base amount for the first fiscal year beginning after the repeal date stated in the SCHEDULE, adjusted thereafter by the applicable percentage increase under section 1886(b)(3)(B)(i) of the Social Security Act.** **The base amount shall equal the aggregate reduction in payments applied under section 1886(p) in the last full fiscal year before the repeal date.** The transfer is permanent and requires no further appropriation.\n\n**(c) Rule of construction.** **This Section reduces no payment to which any individual is entitled under title XVIII, and alters no obligation of the Federal Hospital Insurance Trust Fund.** It ends a reduction in payments to hospitals and applies the same amounts to compensating patients injured in the course of health care.\n\n**(d) What is not affected.** Nothing in this Section affects section 1886(d)(4)(D) of the Social Security Act.\n\n**(e) Relation to the hospital quality incentive.** Where section 6-13 is in effect, the aggregate payment adjustment under that section shall be computed **net of the transfer required by subsection (b)**, so that the transfer is made and the quality incentive is budget neutral with respect to the remainder. **The transfer is not defeated by the budget neutrality of that section, and that section is not defeated by the transfer.**\n\n**(f) If section 6-13 is not enacted.** Subsection (b) operates on the aggregate reduction applied under section 1886(p) in the last full fiscal year before the transfer begins, and section 1886(p) is repealed on that date."},{"anchor":"sec-10-17a","heading":"\u00a7 10-17A. Tax treatment","part":null,"body":"**(a) Compensation \u2014 excluded by force of this subsection.** **Gross income does not include amounts paid to or on behalf of a claimant under \u00a7 10-6**, whether paid periodically or in a single payment, and whether under \u00a7 10-6(a), (b) or (c).\n\n**(a-2) Conforming amendment.** Section 104(a) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended by inserting after paragraph (1) the following: **\"(1A) amounts received under section 10-6 of the Patient Safety and Compensation Act;\"**.\n\n**(b) No offset against benefits.** Amounts paid under \u00a7 10-6 shall not be taken into account as income or resources in determining eligibility for, or the amount of, any Federal program, or any State program financed in whole or part with Federal funds.\n\n**(c) Assessments and the alignment charge.** **Amounts paid under \u00a7 10-15(d), \u00a7 10-15(e) and \u00a7 10-16 are ordinary and necessary business expenses deductible under section 162 of that Code.**\n\n**(c-1) Section 162(f) does not reach them.** **Section 162(f)(1) disallows a deduction only for an amount paid in relation to the violation of a law, or to an investigation or inquiry into the potential violation of a law.** **No amount under \u00a7 10-15(d), \u00a7 10-15(e) or \u00a7 10-16 is imposed on account of any violation, is conditioned on any finding of fault, or carries any element of scienter** \u2014 **and a determination under \u00a7 10-4(e) that an injury was avoidable is expressly not a determination that any person violated any law or breached any duty. See \u00a7 10-1(b).**\n\n**(c-2) And if it were asserted to reach them.** **To the extent section 162(f)(1) is asserted to apply to any such amount, the amount is paid for restitution and for remediation of harm to identified persons within the meaning of section 162(f)(2), and THIS SUBSECTION CONSTITUTES THE IDENTIFICATION REQUIRED BY THAT PARAGRAPH.** **Amounts under \u00a7 10-15 and \u00a7 10-16 are payable into a fund dedicated by \u00a7 10-15(a) to compensating injured persons, and are not paid to the general account or treasury of the United States, nor for general enforcement or any discretionary purpose.**\n\n**(d) Attorney fees and advocate costs.** Amounts paid under \u00a7 10-11 are not included in the gross income of the claimant."},{"anchor":"sec-10-18","heading":"\u00a7 10-18. General","part":null,"body":"**(a) Continuity.** The duties in \u00a7\u00a7 10-5(a), 10-6(a), 10-6(c), and 10-14(a) shall be performed without regard to any lapse in appropriations. **The duties of \u00a7\u00a7 10-4, 10-8, 10-9 and 10-11 \u2014 the review panels, reconsideration, the Court and the Office of Advocates \u2014 are performed without regard to any lapse in appropriations on the same terms.** **The officers and employees necessary to perform those duties, including the special masters, panel members, advocates and clerks shall be designated as excepted from furlough during a lapse in appropriations, identified as such in the published shutdown plan of the agency concerned.**\n\n**(b) Anti-substitution.** Where a payment is made or withheld under any other authority in a manner that would defeat \u00a7 10-16(g) or \u00a7 10-15(f), the officer taking the action shall publish a statement identifying both authorities and the difference between their conditions.\n\n**(c) No charging or disciplinary effect.** **This Section confers no authority to initiate, decline, or influence any prosecution or disciplinary proceeding, reaches no conclusion as to any person's liability, and leaves the authority of every licensing board and every prosecuting authority untouched.**\n\n**(c-1) Applicability.** This Section applies to injuries occurring on or after the date specified in the SCHEDULE. **It does not apply to an injury occurring before that date, and \u00a7 10-3(a) imposes no precondition on an action arising from such an injury.**\n\n**(c-2) The record of assessment.** In setting an assessment under \u00a7 10-15(d) or (e), the Secretary shall publish a statement that no estimate of receipts under \u00a7 10-16 was taken into account. **An assessment set without that statement is of no effect.**\n\n**(d) Severability.** If any provision of this Section, or its application, is held invalid, the remainder is unaffected. **In particular: the invalidity of \u00a7 10-3(a) as applied to actions in the courts of any State does not affect its application to actions in the courts of the United States, and does not affect any other provision of this Section.**\n\n**(e) A knowingly false claim.** \u2014 A claim made with knowledge that it is false is not payable, and any amount paid on it is recoverable. **No other consequence attaches by reason of this Section, and an unsuccessful claim made in good faith carries none.**"},{"anchor":"sec-0-6","heading":"\u00a7 0-6. References to sections of related Acts","part":null,"body":"**(a) SCOPE.** \u2014 A reference in this Act to a section not contained in this Act is a reference to that section of the One Medicare Act, the Medicare Covers Teeth, Eyes, and Ears Act, the Long-Term Care Act, the Public Option Act, the Lowest Drug Price Wins Act, the Medical Care PAID Act, the Keeping Hospitals Open Act, and the Ending Surprise Ambulance Bills and Fake ERs Act, as the context requires.\n\n**(b) IF THE RELATED ACT IS NOT IN EFFECT.** \u2014 Where a section referred to under subsection (a) is not in effect, the reference applies as that section read on the date of enactment of this Act.\n\n**(c) IF THE SECTION HAS NEVER TAKEN EFFECT.** \u2014 Where a provision of this Act conditions a consequence on a report, program, payment, or determination established by a section not contained in this Act, and that section has never taken effect, **the condition is inapplicable and the remainder of the provision operates according to its terms.**\n\n**(d) THIS ACT STANDS ALONE.** \u2014 No provision of this Act depends for its operation on the enactment of any other Act, and the failure of any other Act to be enacted does not affect the operation of this Act."},{"anchor":"sec-0-7","heading":"\u00a7 0-7. Severability","part":null,"body":"**(a)** If any provision of this Act, or the application of any provision to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder of this Act and the application of the provision to any other person or circumstance are not affected.\n\n**(b) NOT ONE INTEGRATED SCHEME.** \u2014 The provisions of this Act are separate and independent. **A court shall not treat this Act as a single integrated scheme, and shall not infer from the invalidity of one provision that the Congress would not have enacted the remainder.**"},{"anchor":"sec-0-8","heading":"\u00a7 0-8. Effective dates","part":null,"body":"**(a) IN GENERAL.** \u2014 Except as otherwise provided, this Act takes effect on the first day of the first plan year beginning after the date that is one year after the date of enactment.\n\n**(b) SCHEDULE VALUES.** \u2014 Where a provision refers to an amount, period, threshold, or rate set in the schedule, that value is the value stated in the SCHEDULE to this Act."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"| Ref | Provision | Value | *(Reserved.)* |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S-1 | \u00a7 10-2(c) threshold period, from the date in S-40 until the first decrease under S-3 | 6 months | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-2 | \u00a7 10-2(c) threshold impairment, from the date in S-40 until the first decrease under S-3 | 10 percent, as assessed under the schedule of severity published under \u00a7 10-6(e) | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-3 | \u00a7 10-2(d) schedule of decreases | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-4 | \u00a7 10-2(d) floor | threshold period: 4 days; threshold impairment: any permanent impairment, whether or not the schedule of severity rates it above zero | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-5 | \u00a7 10-2(d-1) terminal date by which the floor in S-4 is reached | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-6 | \u00a7 10-2(e) pause period | 1 year | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-7 | \u00a7 10-2(e) maximum cumulative pause | 2 years | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-8 | \u00a7\u00a7 10-3(d), 10-10(a)(1) determination period | 240 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-9 | \u00a7 10-3(f) screening period | 30 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-10 | \u00a7 10-4(c-2) community term | 18 months | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-11 | \u00a7 10-4(c-2) number of panels on which a member may sit in a term | 36 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-12 | \u00a7 10-4(c-3)(1) per-diem floor | $60, adjusted with effect on each anniversary of the date of enactment by multiplying the amount then in effect by one plus the measure in S-20, expressed as a decimal fraction, and never reduced | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-13 | \u00a7 10-4(c-3)(2) percentage of the national average weekly wage | 200 percent, the national average weekly wage being that determined under section 6(b)(3) of the Longshore and Harbor Workers' Compensation Act (33 U.S.C. 906(b)(3)) | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-14 | \u00a7 10-4(d) conflict period | 2 years | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-15 | \u00a7 10-6(b) replacement rate | 100 percent of pre-injury after-tax earnings as defined in \u00a7 10-6(b-1) | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-16 | \u00a7 10-6(b) periodic maximum | none | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-17 | \u00a7 10-6(b) reassessment interval | 3 years | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-18 | \u00a7 10-6(c) payment period | 30 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-19 | \u00a7 10-6(e) revision interval | 1 year | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-20 | \u00a7\u00a7 10-6(e-4), 10-15(d) and 10-15(d)(4) general earnings measure | the wage term of the Middle-Class Growth Rate, as defined in \u00a7 1-1(a)(1) of the People's Prosperity Act and certified under \u00a7 1-3(a-1) of that Act, the value so certified not being altered by any later revision of the underlying data, and the value applied in an adjustment being the one most recently certified before the adjustment takes effect; for an adjustment before which no such value has been certified within the preceding fifteen months, including any adjustment taking effect while the People's Prosperity Act is not in effect, the percentage change between the two most recent determinations, before the adjustment takes effect, of the national average weekly wage under section 6(b)(3) of the Longshore and Harbor Workers' Compensation Act (33 U.S.C. 906(b)(3)) | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-21 | \u00a7 10-6(g) cure period | 30 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-22 | \u00a7 10-7(a) election period | 90 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-23 | \u00a7 10-9(b)(1) number of judges | 7 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-24 | \u00a7 10-9(b)(1) judicial term | 15 years | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-25 | \u00a7 10-9(b)(2) master term | 4 years | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-26 | \u00a7 10-9(b)(3) reporting period | 30 days after the publication under \u00a7 10-9(f) that shows the excess | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-27 | \u00a7 10-10(a)(2) reconsideration period | 60 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-28 | \u00a7 10-10(a)(3) master period | 90 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-29 | \u00a7 10-11A(b)(1) intake notice period | 2 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-30 | \u00a7 10-11A(b)(2), (b-0) triggered notice period | 7 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-31 | \u00a7 10-11A(e-4) post-knowledge period | 36 months | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-32 | \u00a7 10-14(b) methodology review interval | 1 year | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-33 | \u00a7 10-15(d) base period | the 3 calendar years ending before the date of enactment, averaged, as determined and published by the Secretary, with its sources and method, before the first assessment under \u00a7 10-15(d) | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-34 | \u00a7 10-15(d)(2) schedule on which risk classes derived from determinations replace insurer classes | the first assessment year beginning after the end of the third full calendar year following the first determination under \u00a7 10-4(e); the replacement is complete in that year | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-35 | \u00a7 10-15(e) basis of the health plan assessment | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-36 | \u00a7 10-15(f) reserve | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-37 | \u00a7 10-16(a) coinsurance rate | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-38 | \u00a7 10-16(b) aggregate cap | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-38A | \u00a7 10-16(d)(3) self-report period | 30 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-39 | \u00a7 10-17(b) repeal date | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-40 | \u00a7 10-18(c-1) applicability date | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |"}],"asOf":"2026-11-15"}}}};
